Licensing

rgx is licensed under the **Apache License, Version 2.0** (Apache-2.0), and
under nothing else. There is no second option to choose from: rgx's own code
is Apache-2.0, and the per-file SPDX header says so.

    a file headed `SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0`  is Apache-2.0

Every tracked `.rs` file carries an SPDX header, and the audit that holds
the claim up is `tests/cli.rs::every_file_carries_its_spdx_header`, which
reads the file list out of git and fails on the first file that carries a
different licence or none at all. Every package in the workspace declares
`license = "Apache-2.0"` to the same effect.

There is no exception to that rule. Where a file implements a layout that
was learned from somebody else's implementation — the borg segment, the
PostgreSQL custom dump, the Redis RDB table, the LMDB meta page, the ACIS
SAT records — what came across is the *layout*: which bytes sit where, and
what they encode. That is a fact about a file format, and no upstream's
licence travels with a fact. The code that encodes it was written here, in
this tree's idiom, and it is held against the corpora each format's own
producer writes. `NOTICE` names every upstream, so a reader who wants to
follow the lineage can.

Why one license
----------------

Apache-2.0 was chosen for the whole tree, and the pack store is where the
decision started: it is the distinctive, independently valuable subsystem of
this project, and Apache-2.0 carries an express patent grant and an explicit
limitation of liability. The codecs that sit under it inherited the same
licence, and when the rest of the tree joined it there was nothing left
under a second one.

The MIT option that used to sit beside it is gone. A project that ships one
licence has one answer to give a reader who asks "under what terms may I use
this?", and "Apache-2.0" is the answer.

What this covers
----------------

    the engine, the walker, the CLI, the recursion pipeline
    every adapter, in every crate under crates/
    the pack store (rgx-core/src/tv/, rgx-core/src/tv_runtime/)
    the compression codecs (rgx-core/src/compress/, the 7z, LZ4 and RAR
    codecs), the zip reader, the base64 and SHA-256 codecs
    the TIFF/EXIF parser and the argument-parser layer
    the tests, the benchmarks, the scripts

Consequence for the distributed binaries
----------------------------------------

The `rgx` binary is built with the pack-store engine linked in by default,
and `rgx-tv` is that engine's own CLI. The default build links the adapter
crates too, and those are Apache-2.0 like the rest of the tree, so the
binary is Apache-2.0 code — plus the Rust dependencies it links, each of
which carries its own permissive licence. Those are notices, not obligations
on this code, and they are named in [`NOTICE`](NOTICE).

Disabling the pack store does not change the licence, only the size of the
binary: `--no-default-features` turns off the `tv` and `tv-runtime` features,
but the codecs, the zip reader and the CLI layer are linked into any build of
`rgx-core`.

What this does not cover
------------------------

Four things in and around this repository are not rgx's code:

  * **The dependencies.** Each crate in `Cargo.lock` carries its own licence.
    They are notices, not obligations on this code, and the set is named in
    [`NOTICE`](NOTICE).

  * **The test fixtures.** The corpora under `crates/*/testdata/` are real
    files, taken from the reference implementations of the formats they
    exercise rather than written by hand, and each is covered by its own
    project's licence — BSD-3-Clause for most of them, Apache-2.0 for the
    HPGL-Plotter capture, and the terms of their own projects for the rest.
    The README of each fixture directory says which file came from which
    project and what its terms are. They are data, not code, and none of
    them carries a licence obligation on this code.

  * **The lineage.** The 7z tree was written from the LZMA SDK, which its
    publisher placed in the public domain; the RAR codec was originally a
    port of libarchive's RAR support (BSD-2-Clause) and every module of it
    has since been rewritten. No upstream source file from either lineage
    remains in this repository. Both lineages are recorded in
    `scripts/vendored-sources.tsv` and in `NOTICE`.

  * **The layouts.** Five files implement a layout that was learned from a
    third-party implementation: the borg segment, the PostgreSQL custom dump,
    the Redis RDB type table, the LMDB meta page and the ACIS SAT records.
    What was taken is the layout, and the code is rgx's own; each upstream is
    named in `NOTICE`.

The *format specifications* the adapters implement are separate works with
their own terms — ITU-T, ISO/IEC, NIST, MIL-STD, W3C/OASIS and others. This
repository implements them; it does not redistribute them, and it grants no
rights in them.

Full text: LICENSE-APACHE. Provenance and third-party notices: NOTICE.
